GoodHelicities class is designed to cycle over a Root TTrain and find the good helicities. A TTrain is a custom class that has a list of TTrees and
files similar to a TChain which keeps track of which file to load and reinitiates all branch address setting when a new file/TTree is loaded. It was
required because TChain could not set the branch addresses for complex variables like fEvtHeader.fEvtTime.

Installation follows the usual CMake process (depends on HelPat and TTrain already being installed and libraries available):
Inside GoodHelicities directory
>mkdir build
>cd build
>cmake ..
>make


struct HelPat_t {
   Long64_t pattern_start{0}; //Entry number of pattern start (last event inside Tsettle)
   Long64_t pattern_end{0};   //Entry number of pattern end (last event before Tsettle)

   std::vector<Long64_t> window_start; //array of entry numbers of helicity window starts (last event inside Tsettle at beginning of helicity window)
   std::vector<Long64_t> window_end;   //array of entry numbers of helicity window starts (last event before next Tsettle)
   std::vector<bool>     helicity;     //array of true helicities (not delayed)

   int  polarity{0};          //polarity of pattern +/-1
   bool complete{false};      //has correct number of helicity states from given pattern
   bool sequenceOK{false};    //has helicity sequence correct for given pattern
   bool timingOK{false};      //timing between windows consistent with 1/TsettleFrequency
   bool predictedOK{false};   //sequence of random helicity patterns consistent with helicity sequence predictor
}

Main methods for GoodHelicities:
GoodHelicities::FindGoodHelicities() Returns a vector of type HelPat that has elements as shown above. This is the method that does the primary
                                     processing. Other methods are used to set up the class before calling this method.
GoodHelicities::SetTsettleFrequency(double freq) set helicity flip frequency. Used to check for missing helicity windows.
GoodHelicities::SetPatternType() "pair","quartet","octet","hexoquad","octoquad" or "toggle"
GoodHelicities::SetDelayWindows() number of windows reported helicity is delayed
GoodHelicities::SetNMaxPulse() sets the maximum number of pulses that the firmware is set to find per event readout (default 4)
GoodHelicities::SetADCthreshold() optionally sets the ADC threshold for finding an MPS, QRT or helicity
GoodHelicities::SetNSA() set number of samples included in pulse integral after threshold crossing (NSA config file parameter). Used only for pedestal subtraction.
GoodHelicities::SetNSB() set number of samples included in pulse integral before threshold crossing (NSB config file parameter). Used only for pedestal subtraction.
GoodHelicities::SetNPED() set number of samples used in finding pedestals. Used only for pedestal subtraction.
GoodHelicities::SetNChannels(double nch) set number of physical FADC channels being read out

There is also an included class::LFSR30 (30 bit linear feedback shift register) for predicting the helicity after initial 30 pattern sequence is given.

Example use:
GoodHelicities *gh = new GoodHelicities();
gh->SetPatterType("quartet");
gh->SetTsettleFreq(29.5596);
gh->SetNchannels(16);
gh->SetNMaxPulse(4);
int run = 249;
+TTrain* tree = new TTrain();
for (int x = 0; x <= 100; ++x) {
    TString filename(Form("%s/%s%i",
                          gSystem->Getenv("HAMOLLER_ROOTFILE_DIR"),
                          fnameform, run));
    if (x > 0) filename += Form("_%i", x);
    filename += ".root";

    cout << filename << endl;

    if (gSystem->AccessPathName(filename)) break;

    tree->AddFile(filename, "T");
}
gh->FindGoodHelicities(tree);
const vector<HelPat_t>& patterns = gh->GetPatterns();
